\section{Sample accounting}{

Analytical tables store a source ledger in \code{meta$sample_accounting}.
It supplements the displayed statistics with record counts and their
evidence. Legacy and hand-built tables may omit it. \code{as.data.frame()}
carries the ledger in a \code{sample_accounting} attribute.

The ledger is a list with \code{version = 1L} and three data frames:
\itemize{
\item \code{populations}: \code{id}, \code{component}, \code{command}, \code{scope}, \code{variable},
\code{group}, \code{model}, \code{imputation}, \code{spec}, \code{weight_type}. IDs are unique.
Scope is \code{table}, \code{group}, \code{variable}, \code{model}, \code{imputation},
\code{evaluation} or \code{summary}. Model, imputation and specification indices
are positive whole numbers or \code{NA}; other columns are character.
Variable and group may be \code{NA}. Component paths distinguish sources;
model indices remain local to their component.
\item \code{measures}: \code{population_id}, \code{metric}, \code{value}, \code{status}, \code{basis},
\code{reason}. Every population has each metric below exactly once.
Status is \code{available}, \code{unavailable} or \code{not_applicable}. Available
values are finite and nonnegative. Unknown or inapplicable values
are \code{NA} with a reason; basis identifies the input or stored evidence.
\item \code{exclusions}: \code{population_id}, \code{stage}, \code{reason}, \code{n}, \code{status},
\code{basis}. Optional reason counts use stages \code{input_to_eligible},
\code{eligible_to_used} or \code{retained}. Retained reasons describe missing
categories or events intentionally included by the command. Counts
are whole records or explicitly unavailable; overlapping reasons
cannot establish a unique excluded population.
}

Record metrics are \code{input_n} (before this population's filtering),
\code{eligible_n} (shared eligibility), \code{observed_n} (nonmissing variable or
outcome), \code{used_n} (contributors under the command's policy),
\code{fitted_n} (fitted-model contributors, excluding known zero weights),
\code{frame_n} (stored representation rows), \code{missing_n}, \code{zero_weight_n},
and \code{excluded_n} (input minus used when both describe the same scope).
Frame rows can include zero weights or expanded Cox risk sets.
A variable population can start with an already eligible group;
earlier exclusions then belong to its parent. Retained missing values
can make used exceed observed, and zero-weight counts can describe
the original input rather than the eligible sample.

\code{weight_sum} uses original-scale contributor weights; \code{effective_n}
is a Kish record-weight diagnostic, not survey-design ESS or model
degrees of freedom. \code{reported_n} preserves the displayed or native N,
whose basis can be frequency totals, events, trials or records.
These three metrics may be fractional. Unrepresentable weight totals
and unrecoverable counts remain unavailable.

Fitted-model metadata uses stored evidence without evaluating fitting
calls. Original input counts are often unavailable. Effect-evaluation
grids are separate from fitted samples. Composition preserves each
source population, including reused sources and display-row selections;
it does not sum groups, models or imputations into a unique cohort N.
Supplied summaries and workbook-only sources cannot establish record
counts. Generated ledgers contain no observation IDs, observation row
names or raw data.
Malformed supplied ledgers raise \code{tabtools_error_sample_accounting}.
}

